            "isTitleSlide": false,
            "isSectionDivider": false,
            "rawHtml": "<h3>03 -- VLANS: VLANs Were Never Built for Segmentation</h3><p class=\"lead-statement\">VLANs Were Never Built for Segmentation VLANs were originally designed to reduce network congestion -- not to control security communications. Using</p><div class=\"info-card mt-3\"><p>them for segmentation is, in many ways, a misuse of the technology. Doesn&#x27;t extend to the cloud VLANs and traditional ACLs can&#x27;t reach cloud workloads directly -- you end up bolting on SDN and third-party virtualized firewalls just to cover the gap. Containers stay exposed Every container on a host shares the same kernel. One exploited container can put every other container on that host at risk -- VLANs have no answer for this. Hits a hard protocol ceiling VLANs cap out at 4,096 segments -- a real constraint in a large data center where far more granular boundaries are needed. NETWORK SEGMENTATION &amp; MICROSEGMENTATION 10</p></div>",
