            "eyebrow": "CIT 220 • Unit 02.2",
            "subtitle": "Approach Three: Microsegmentation to Layer 7 & Ransomware Defense",
            "lead": "EXAMPLE SCENARIO A Ransomware Attempt, Layer by Layer Tracing one attack attempt through all three segmentation approaches shows why depth matters more than any single control.",
            "isTitleSlide": false,
            "isSectionDivider": false,
            "rawHtml": "<h3>EXAMPLE SCENARIO</h3><p class=\"lead-statement\">A Ransomware Attempt, Layer by Layer Tracing one attack attempt through all three segmentation approaches shows why depth matters more than any single control.</p><div class=\"info-card mt-3\"><p>01 Initial access Phishing email compromises a single endpoint in the corporate environment segment. 02 Environment segmentation holds The compromised endpoint sits in the corporate segment CDE environments stay separate. 03 Attacker tries east-west spread Malware attempts to reach application servers by piggybacking on an open Layer 4 port. 04 Layer 7 policy blocks itThe process attempting the connection doesn&#x27;t match the allowlisted, verified hash -- traffic is denied by default. 05 Full visibility capturedThe attempted flow, process hash, and timing are logged in real time for the security team and for compliance evidence. Any single layer might have missed this. Layered, combined segmentation caught it. NETWORK SEGMENTATION &amp; MICROSEGMENTATION 24</p></div><ul class=\"compact mt-3\"><li>production and</li></ul>",
            "cleanText": "EXAMPLE SCENARIO A Ransomware Attempt, Layer by Layer Tracing one attack attempt through all three segmentation approaches shows why depth matters more than any single control. 01 Initial access Phishing email compromises a single endpoint in the corporate environment segment. 02 Environment segmentation holds The compromised endpoint sits in the corporate segment -- production and CDE environments stay separate. 03 Attacker tries east-west spread Malware attempts to reach application servers by piggybacking on an open Layer 4 port. 04 Layer 7 policy blocks itThe process attempting the connection doesn't match the allowlisted, verified hash -- traffic is denied by default. 05 Full visibility capturedThe attempted flow, process hash, and timing are logged in real time for the security team and for compliance evidence. Any single layer might have missed this. Layered, combined segmentation caught it. NETWORK SEGMENTATION & MICROSEGMENTATION 24",
